p(11,19,"I consider,” said Sir Oliver, “that blinkers are dangerous things",
 "Sir Oliver says that blinkers are dangerous at night because horses can see better in the dark than men. He gives two examples of accidents that blinkers may have caused.",
 "Sir Oliver says that he thinks blinkers are dangerous things at night. Horses can see much better in the dark than men can, and many accidents would not have happened if horses had the full use of their eyes. He remembers that some years ago a hearse with two horses was coming back one dark night, by Farmer Sparrow’s house, where the pond is close to the road. The wheels went too near the edge, and the hearse fell over into the water. Both horses drowned, and the driver only just got away. After this accident they put up a strong white rail that could be seen easily. But if those horses had not been partly blind, they would have stayed further from the edge themselves, and there would have been no accident. When their master’s carriage fell over, before Black Beauty came, people said that John would have seen the big hole that the road-makers had left if the lamp on the left side had not gone out. Sir Oliver says that may be so, but if old Colin had not had blinkers on, he would have seen the hole with or without a lamp, because he was far too clever an old horse to run into danger. As it was, Colin was badly hurt and the carriage was broken, and nobody knew how John got away.",
 ["blinkers","hearse","overturned","stout","rail","blinded","road-makers","knowing"])
s(11,"if the lamp on the left side had not gone out",
 "If the lamp on the left side had stayed lit, John would have seen the big hole.",
 "'if ... had not gone out, John would have seen' is the third conditional: it talks about something in the past that did not happen.")
