p(12,14,"So do I,” said master, “as well as any man",
 "The master says he likes horses to hold their heads up, but not when the head is held up by force. He says horses were meant to have their heads free, and fashion is a poor guide.",
 "The master says that he likes to see horses hold their heads up as much as any man does, but he does not like to see them held up. That takes away all the pleasure. He tells Langley, who is a military man, that Langley likes to see his soldiers look good on parade, with their heads up. But he would not be proud of their drill if all his men had their heads tied to a board. It might do little harm on parade, except to worry and tire them. But in a charge with bayonets against the enemy, when they need to use every muscle and push all their strength forward, he would not give much for their chance of winning. It is the same with horses. If you worry them and make them upset, you take away their power. You do not let them throw their weight against their work, so they have to do too much with their joints and muscles, and it wears them out faster. You can be sure that horses were meant to have their heads free, as free as men’s heads. If people acted a little more by common sense and a good deal less by fashion, many things would work more easily. And if a horse makes a wrong step, it has much less chance to get back on its feet if its head and neck are fastened back. The master laughs and says that he has taken his hobby out for a good trot, and asks whether the captain cannot make up his mind to get on it too, because his example would go a long way.",
 ["regiment","parade","drill","backboard","bayonet","muscle","fatigue","hobby","trot"])
s(12,"you would not take much credit for your drill if all your men had their heads tied to a backboard",
 "You would not be proud of how well you trained your men if they all had their heads tied to a board.",
 "'if all your men had their heads tied' uses the past tense after 'if' for an imagined case, and 'you would not take much credit' is its result.") if False else None
s(12,"you would not take much credit for your drill",
 "You would not get much praise for how well your men drill.",
 "'would not take' with the 'if ... had' part that follows is an imagined case: it says what would happen if something untrue were true.") if False else None
s(12,"if a horse makes a false step, he has much less chance",
 "If a horse takes a wrong step, he has much less chance to get his balance back.",
 "'recovering himself' uses the reflexive 'himself' after the -ing form to mean 'getting back his balance'; 'of' links 'chance' to the action.")
